faq:
  - q: What do I receive today versus at settlement?
    a: Under a simple structure, the advance is paid when the factor buys an eligible invoice. The reserve is released later, after the broker or shipper pays, minus the factoring fee if the fee comes out of that reserve. Some agreements deduct the fee from the advance instead. Choose the structure that matches your contract.
  - q: Should the advance and the reserve add up to 100%?
    a: Often they do, because the reserve is the part of the invoice that was not advanced. If your percentages do not add up to 100, the calculator will flag that. Read the agreement rather than forcing the numbers to fit.
  - q: Are the starting percentages typical factoring rates?
    a: No. The invoice amount, 90% advance, 3% fee, and 10% reserve are examples so the calculator has something to show when the page opens. They are not a quote and not a market average.
  - q: Is factoring a loan?
    a: Factoring is the sale of an eligible invoice under a contract, not a loan in the ordinary sense. Recourse, non-recourse, and chargeback language still decide who holds the risk if the invoice is unpaid or short-paid. This calculator does not interpret those terms.
