<div class="calculator" id="calculator" data-calculator="factoring-cost" role="group" aria-labelledby="calculator-title">
  <h2 id="calculator-title">The calculator</h2>
  <p class="field-hint">The starting numbers are examples, not a factoring quote or a typical rate.</p>
  <div class="calculator-panel">
    <div>
      <label for="fac-invoice">Invoice amount (dollars)</label>
      <input id="fac-invoice" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.01" value="2500">
      <p class="field-hint">Example: 2,500.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="fac-advance">Advance rate (percent)</label>
      <input id="fac-advance" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="100" step="0.1" value="90">
      <p class="field-hint">Example: 90. Not a quoted advance.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="fac-fee">Factoring fee (percent)</label>
      <input id="fac-fee" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="100" step="0.1" value="3">
      <p class="field-hint">Example: 3. Not a typical or recommended fee.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="fac-reserve">Reserve (percent)</label>
      <input id="fac-reserve" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="100" step="0.1" value="10">
      <p class="field-hint">Example: 10. Often the share that was not advanced.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="field-span choice-group">
      <p id="fac-timing-label">When is the fee deducted?</p>
      <div class="choice-row" role="radiogroup" aria-labelledby="fac-timing-label">
        <label><input type="radio" name="fac-timing" value="reserve" checked> From the reserve, at settlement</label>
        <label><input type="radio" name="fac-timing" value="advance"> From the advance, today</label>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="calculator-actions">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-calculate>Calculate</button>
    </div>
    <div class="calculator-result" data-result aria-live="polite">
      <h3>Estimate</h3>
      <p class="calculator-error" data-error></p>
      <div class="result-grid">
        <div>
          <p>Today (the advance)</p>
          <strong data-today></strong>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>At settlement (the reserve, after the fee if taken then)</p>
          <strong data-later></strong>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>Factoring fee</p>
          <strong data-fee></strong>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>Total of the two payments</p>
          <strong data-total></strong>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="calculator-note" data-note hidden></p>
      <p class="calculator-note">This is an estimate from the percentages you entered. It is not a quote, and it does not account for chargebacks, short pays, or extra fees in a contract.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<article class="prose">
  <h2 id="advance-fee-and-reserve">Advance, fee, and reserve</h2>
  <p>Freight factoring is designed to close the gap between delivery and a broker’s payment terms. The carrier sells an eligible invoice to a factor and receives an advance. A reserve is often held back until the broker or shipper pays. A fee, set in the agreement, is deducted from one of those two moments.</p>
  <p>The starting figures here — a $2,500 invoice, a 90% advance, a 3% fee, and a 10% reserve — are examples so the two payment moments are visible when you open the page. They are not a quote and not an average. Use the percentages in your own agreement.</p>
  <p>The fee and the reserve are not the whole contract. Recourse, non-recourse, and chargeback language decide what happens if the invoice is unpaid or disputed. That is covered in <a href="/factoring/freight-factoring-recourse-vs-non-recourse/">freight factoring, recourse vs non-recourse</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="two-ways-the-fee-is-taken">When the fee comes out</h2>
  <p>Some agreements deduct the fee from the reserve when the invoice is collected. In the example above, that would mean $2,250 today (90% of $2,500) and $175 at settlement (the $250 reserve minus a $75 fee). Other agreements deduct the fee from the advance, so today is smaller and the reserve is released later without taking the fee a second time.</p>
  <p>Choose the option that matches the contract in front of you. If the advance and reserve do not add up to 100%, the calculator will say so. Do not invent a split that the agreement does not use.</p>
  <p>If a dispatch fee also comes out of the same load, calculate it separately with the <a href="/tools/dispatch-fee/">dispatch fee calculator</a>. The two contracts should be read on their own terms, not blended into a single unofficial percentage.</p>
</article>

